                        "review": "A strong game held back by a weak, confusing opening.\n\nThis game has a lot of strong points.  The graphics and 3D style are fantastic.  Beautiful and unique.  I've never played anything that looks and feels quite like it.  The soundtrack is fantastic, and is nicely integrated into the main story.\n\nThe only negative thing I have to say about it is that it really does flub the opening.  The game opens with a confusing sequence that doesn't really offer you anything to grab on to, and once that ends, you're dumped into a sequence that hints that the entire game will be some sort of a dry, retconed, alternate history that projects modern progressive values back onto the year 1851.  Making progress past all that feels like a bit of a slog, and doesn't give the player much reason to assume the entire game won't be more of the same.  But when the story finally gets moving, it REALLY gets moving.  You can actually feel the first moment when something interesting happens, because it's so striking.  \"Wait, what?  Does that mean what I think it means?\"  From then on out, the game is SOLID.\n\nIn fact, from then on out, the game moves so smoothly that the next thing you'll think to complain about is the cliffhanger at the end, as we wait for the conclusion.\n\nIn summary, you should absolutely play this if you're a fan of point & click adventure games with a strong story.  Just be prepared to give this one a very generous chance to get moving.  It will, if you let it.",
